==Evolutionary history==
The geography of ''Salvinia'' fossil material suggests members of the genus may have been broadly distributed during the [[Tertiary period|Tertiary]].<ref name="Wang2014">{{cite journal |author1=Wang, Li |author2=Qing-Qing Xu |author3=Jian-Hua Jin |title=A reconstruction of the fossil Salvinia from the Eocene of Hainan Island, South China |journal=Review of Palaeobotany and Palynology |date=2014 |volume=203 |pages=12–21 |doi=10.1016/j.revpalbo.2013.12.005 |bibcode=2014RPaPa.203...12W |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0034666714000025|url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref name="MississippiMiocene">{{cite journal |author1=McNair, D.M. |author2=D.Z. Stults |author3=B. Axsmith |author4=M.H. Alford |author5=J.E. Starnes |title=Preliminary investigation of a diverse megafossil floral assemblage from the middle Miocene of southern Mississippi, USA |journal=[[Palaeontologia Electronica]] |date=2019 |volume=22 |issue=2 |doi=10.26879/906 |url=https://palaeo-electronica.org/content/pdfs/906.pdf|doi-access=free }}</ref>

==Distribution==
Distribution is mostly tropical, in [[North America]], [[Mexico]], [[West Indies]], [[Central America]], [[South America]], [[Eurasia]] and [[Africa]], including [[Madagascar]], [[South Borneo]], [[Asia]].

==Economic impacts==
Giant salvinia (''[[Salvinia molesta]]'') is a commonly introduced [[noxious weed|invasive weed]] in warm climates, but is native to South America. It grows rapidly, up to two times its dry weight in {{frac|2|1|2}} days, and forms dense mats over still waters. A tiny [[weevil]], ''[[Cyrtobagous salviniae]]'', has been used successfully to biologically control giant salvinia.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ward |first=Colin |date=2013-09-05 |title=Salvinia biocontrol |url=https://csiropedia.csiro.au/salvinia-biocontrol/ |access-date=2022-08-28 |website=CSIROpedia |language=en-AU}}</ref>

One proposed use takes advantage of the hydrophobic [[trichome]]s, which do not repel oil. This makes them candidates for mopping up oil spills, as they become saturated with oil in thirty seconds. ''[[Salvinia molesta|S. molesta]]'' [[trichome]]s served as a model for a similarly hydrophobic synthetic [[polycarbonate]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://newatlas.com/nanofur-fern-oil-spill/45076|title=Oil spill clean-up material functions like a fern|last=Coxworth|first=Ben|date=August 24, 2016|website=newatlas.com|publisher=|access-date=2016-08-25}}</ref>

==Salvinia effect==
The [[salvinia effect]] describes the stabilization of an air layer upon a submerged [[Hydrophobe|hydrophobic]] (water-repellent) surface by [[Hydrophile|hydrophilic]] (water loving) pins. This physic-chemical phenomenon was discovered on the floating fern ''Salvinia molesta'' by the botanist [[Wilhelm Barthlott]] ([[University of Bonn]]) while working on the [[lotus effect]] and was described in cooperation with the physicist Thomas Schimmel ([[Karlsruhe Institute of Technology]]), fluid mechanist Alfred Leder ([[University of Rostock]]) and their colleagues in 2010.

